      "item": "lem-tight-borel-gaussian-law-has-separable-support",
      "finding": "In lem-tight-borel-gaussian-law-has-separable-support, step 2.1 says: “The same map W is Borel as an E-valued map because the Borel sets of E are intersections of E with Borel sets of B.” The construction proves only μ(E)=1, so the original W can take values outside E on a nonempty measurable null event. For example, on a complete probability space let W=0 off one null singleton and W=v≠0 on that singleton; its law is δ₀ and all scalar coordinates are degenerate centered normal. The proof permits K_m={0}, hence E={0}, but this same W is not E-valued. An almost-sure modification is needed to justify the asserted E-valued random element.",
